The  is the in‐kernel bootloader for platforms that do not have a
proper firmware.  Some platforms  supported  by  do  not  have  a
firmware  that  can  boot the kernel.  Examples are game consoles
(dreamcast  and  playstation2  ports),  and  handhelds   (hpcarm,
hpcmips,  and  hpcsh ports).  On such platforms the bootloader is
usually a host program that runs under the native OS.  This means
that rebooting is a lengthy process of booting into the native OS
first, launching the  bootloader  program,  and  finally  booting
again.   This  problem is addressed by which allows the currently
running kernel to serve as a  bootloader  for  the  kernel  being
booted,  thus  avoiding  the burden of booting into the native OS
first.  When is configured into the kernel, a call to causes  the
to load the new kernel into memory, and arrange for control to be
passed  to  the  new  kernel  — just like a standalone bootloader
does.  The new kernel then boots in the ordinary  manner.   first
